## Item 1 — Terms and Conditions of Purchase (August 2026)

The client sent a *Terms and Conditions of Purchase* template plus a
checkout screenshot showing the "I accept the Terms and Conditions"
checkbox above the Place Order button. The client believes it came from
the payment processor.

⚠️ The client described this as coming from "our new processor".
Items 2–4 below came from **AllayPay** underwriting. If underwriting has
moved to a different provider, confirm who, and re-check that items 2–4
still reflect that provider's asks — they are defensible on their own
merits, but the attribution in this doc would be wrong.

**What the template was:** a fill-in-the-blank skeleton, not a finished
contract. It shipped with `[Merchant Name]`, `MerchantWebsite.com`,
`[insert]` for the governing-law state, and two slots reading
"*to be inserted based on merchant's industry vertical / terms*".

**Implementation:** merged into the existing Terms of Service (page 29,
`/terms` — the page `woocommerce_terms_page_id` points at, and therefore
the page behind the checkout checkbox). Content is version-controlled at
`docs/legal/terms-of-service.html` and pushed with
`scripts/terms-update-wpcom.sh`. Every template section is accounted for;
`docs/legal/terms-coverage-map.md` is the clause-by-clause map, including
the four clauses that were reworded and why.

The two "to be inserted" slots were filled as the client directed:

- **§3 Product and sale limitations** — the client's supplied text,
  verbatim.
- **§8 Returns and refunds** — reproduces the live `/refund-policy`
  page (ID 13) in full.

⚠️ The client's §3 text intentionally contains "diagnosis, treatment,
prevention, cure" — terms banned everywhere else on the site (see
`docs/COMPLIANCE` → Prohibited Language). As with the FDA disclaimer in
item 2, they are permitted ONLY inside this canonical, client-supplied
clause. Do not edit, paraphrase, or scrub it.

Note this text is a *different* RUO formulation from
`nav_get_disclaimer('sitewide')`. The sitewide line and its JSON-LD
`disambiguatingDescription` counterpart are unchanged — the processor
scanner cross-check still passes. §3 is an additional purchase-terms
clause, not a replacement, and is deliberately not routed through
`nav_get_disclaimer()`: it lives in page content, and a shortcode
indirection to protect one paragraph was judged not worth the machinery.

**ACH:** the template's two ACH/e-Check debit authorizations were
included (§6), payable to "Navigate Peptides". No ACH gateway is
installed on production yet — the clauses are conditional on placing an
ACH/e-Check order, so they are dormant rather than inaccurate. Revisit
the payable-to line and the footer payment marks when ACH goes live.

